      "title": "cv2.quality: full-reference vs no-reference",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "Grading images with numbers instead of eyes (cv2.quality).\n\nFULL-REFERENCE (section A) needs the original. That is the situation whenever YOU made the degradation: denoiser settings, an upscaler, a codec, a resize. 'CV Quality Compare Batch' takes one reference and a BATCH of candidates, computes the reference's internal state ONCE and scores every frame against it - a parameter sweep in one node. Mind the direction: SSIM and PSNR are higher-is-better, MSE and GMSD are lower-is-better. 'best_index' has already applied it, and 'higher_is_better' comes out as a BOOLEAN so a graph can rank without hardcoding the metric.\nThe scores are a picture of NUMBERS - chart them, do not preview them as pixels. The quality MAPS are a picture of pixels: they show WHERE a candidate lost, which a single score cannot. (GMSD's map is half resolution - the metric works on a downsampled gradient map.)\n\nNO-REFERENCE (section B) is the case that matches a real capture: there is no ground truth, only the frame you got. BRISQUE fits an SVM over 36 natural-scene-statistics features and returns 0 (best) to 100 (worst) from the image ALONE - which is how you drop a soft frame out of a burst, or refuse to run a pipeline on a bad input. It needs two data files in ComfyUI/models/quality (brisque_model_live.yml and brisque_range_live.yml, from opencv_contrib modules/quality/samples). The node returns 'worst_index', and the batch is indexed with it here so the picture proves the number.\n\nSection C exposes the 36 features themselves. They need no model file, and they are a generic descriptor: wire 'features' into 'CV Train Classifier' to learn YOUR notion of quality (in-focus vs soft, clean vs compressed) instead of the LIVE database's.\n\nTry: switch the metric to PSNR and watch the winner stay the same but the scale change completely; blur the ORIGINAL too and see the full-reference scores stay high (it only knows 'close to the reference') while BRISQUE's rise - that difference IS the reason both families exist."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
